  [3mBuys:[0m one place that owns the storage key and the record shape, so swapping to a server-issued id later is a one-line
  change. Matches the repo's existing loading model exactly, no build step, still works opened straight from disk.
  [3mCosts:[0m relies on a global and on script order in the HTML. Not importable by a Node test runner without a shim.

  [1mB. Convert the browser side to ES modules[0m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m, a real [38;5;153msession.js[39m with [38;5;153mexport[39ms,
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m imports it.
  [3mBuys:[0m a genuine module boundary with no globals and no load-order coupling, and the same import syntax the future
  forms would use.
  [3mCosts:[0m [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is blocked by CORS on [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so the page would have to be served over HTTP from then on — a
  real workflow change in a repo with no dev server or build step. It also adds a third module convention alongside
  [38;5;153msrc/[39m's CommonJS.

  [1mC. Inline [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m calls, no module[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m does [38;5;153msessionStorage.setItem(...)[39m directly; future forms call
  [38;5;153mgetItem[39m themselves.
  [3mBuys:[0m the smallest diff by far.
  [3mCosts:[0m the storage key and value shape become an implicit contract copy-pasted into every future consumer. Since
  you've said other forms will need this, this is the option that ages worst — and it forfeits the single swap point you
  picked in the last question.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m It gets you the thing that actually matters from your answers — one owner for the stored identity, so
  the later move to a real server id touches one file — without B's requirement that the page now be served over HTTP.
  If you'd rather have proper ES modules and don't mind running a local server, B is the better long-term shape and I'd
  happily take it; C I'd argue against, because it contradicts the swap point you just chose.
